title The room that shipped nothing
prompt Keaton: adapt our publish seed scrub script to allow the gratitude folder in the os and ww seeds, just without my personal information.
think Lap B of eight. The manifest already gave gratitude a template verdict, so the first read said this was one line. It was not: gratitude is not on the ALLOWLIST, and the allowlist is the ship driver.
think Reading the room decided the verdict rather than the ask did. Twenty of its 132 tracked files name the maintainer, a handle or a pier path, so the verdict is scrub rather than template -- a thanks record ships depersonalized rather than not at all. Two classes are withheld and neither is about privacy: the saved third-party article is COPYRIGHT, and all twenty gitlinks are LICENCE, since the whole point of a gitlink is that our history never carries their source.
think The gitlink safety was INCIDENTAL and I made it structural. sow.rish copies files and knows nothing of gitlinks; the submodules are unchecked-out on this pier, so today it ships empty directories. A hand who checks one out for a read would turn that into a leak, so each is named by path.
obs THE ALLOWLIST HAD A SECOND LIST QUIETLY SUBTRACTING FROM IT, AND EVERY GUARD SAID GREEN. sow_project.sh filtered the allowlist through grep -vxE gratitude|vendor -- a hard exclusion by NAME, running after it. allow gratitude read as effective, the projection answered copied=7603, sow_witness answered GREEN, and ls seed/gratitude read 0. A room that ships no bytes leaks no names, so IDENT_CLEAN was a true statement about the wrong corpus. Booked %489 -- first written as %485 and renumbered when a rebase found three peer rows published in the same hour.
obs I ONLY CAUGHT IT BY COUNTING THE SHIPPED DIRECTORY. Every instrument I had said the change worked. The one thing that disagreed was ls. That is the same habit %409 was booked for and the reason the fix is a guard rather than an edit: sow_witness asks did anything private get out, seed_link asks does a shipped link land, and neither can ask did the room we allowed arrive.
obs THE NEW GUARD FOUND A SECOND CASE ON ITS FIRST RUN, AND THE SECOND CASE TAUGHT IT THE RIGHT SHAPE. context/LEXICON.md is allowed and never ships: an identity string survives the scrub and defence in depth drops the copy. That is the fail-safe working, and counting it would make the gate red on correct behaviour. So the gate turns on absent AND UNLOGGED, and a logged absence is reported as withheld_by_design. The seed's readers have no Lexicon and nothing said so -- a lap of its own.
obs THE PIN NEEDED THE LOOM. REDS stood at 39,840 of 40,960 and the row wanted 1,365. Rows %470 and %471 both read BOOKED while every other row reads OPEN, so the fold loom moved them to a shelf under one clause they genuinely share -- an instrument asking a cheaper question than its name claims. Pin now 37,502.
loom seed copied_before=7603 copied_after=7726 gratitude_entries=0_then_77 withheld=135
loom seed personal_markers_in_shipped_gratitude=0 ident=CLEAN no_personal=yes
loom scan=sow_allow_reach allows=107 shipped=106 withheld_by_design=1 empty=0 elder_filter_empty=1
file template-manifest.bron gratitude allowed and scrubbed; the article withheld by copyright, twenty gitlinks by licence
file tools/fixtures/s/sow_project.sh the second filter keeps vendor alone, with the reason written beside it
file tools/fixtures/s/sow_allow_reach_scan.sh the reading no seed guard had -- did the allowed room arrive
file tools/s/sow_allow_reach_witness.rish the gate, rostered tier lap, proven both ways on real projections
file construction/REDS.md %489 booked (was %485, renumbered on a rebase); %470 and %471 folded to their shelf to make the room
scope lap B of eight -- gratitude ships depersonalized, and the silence that hid its absence is now a rostered guard
status GREEN -- SOW_WITNESS_GREEN with IDENT_CLEAN and NO_PERSONAL, sow_allow_reach empty=0, seed_link ok, reds monotone and fold green
recommend keep-going -- lap C is the scrub-script template with fillable stubs, which the personalize template already half-answers.
